  "worked_solution": "Suppose the quadratic equation\n\n$$\na{x}^{2}+bx+c=0\n$$\n\nhas the solutions $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2},$ where $a\\ne 0.$ \n\nThen, Vieta's formulas state the following:\n\n- The sum of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-\\frac{b}{a}.\n$$\n\n- The product of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{c}{a}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's use the given information to determine the values of $a,b,$ and $c$ in this case:\n\n- We're told that the quadratic coefficient equals $3.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\na=3.\n$$\n\n- We're told that the sum of the roots equals $4.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =-\\frac{b}{a} \\\\ 4 & =-\\frac{b}{3} \\\\ -12 & =b \\\\ b & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- We're told that the product of the roots equals $-5.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5 & =\\frac{c}{a} \\\\ -5 & =\\frac{c}{3} \\\\ -15 & =c \\\\ c & =-15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our equation has coefficients\n\n$$\na=3,\\,b=-12,\\,c=-15\n$$\n\nand the equation is\n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}-12x-15=0.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
